⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the post-create race adoption has no deterministic tie-break — a symmetric race still splits the `defered:` tags across two open digests**

The rework added the lowest-number tie-break to `findExistingDigest` (replays converge — verified by the order-independence test), but the **post-create adoption branch does not apply it**. It adopts *any* concurrent digest unconditionally:

```js
var raced = probeExistingDigest(t, owner, name, input, out, created);
var racedNumber = raced && digestNumberOf(raced);
if (racedNumber) {          // ← adopts regardless of whether the raced digest is EARLIER or LATER than ours
    out.digest = racedNumber;
    out.digestReused = true;
    out.digestDuplicate = created;
    return raced;
}
```

Trace the symmetric window (both funnels' creates land before both post-probes, and the search index has caught up for both — exactly the lag pattern the finding-4 test simulates with its two-phase `searchIssues` mock):

- Funnel A creates **#501**; its post-probe excludes 501, finds B's **#512** → adopts #512, abandons its own #501. A's `defered:` acks point at **#512**.
- Funnel B creates **#512**; its post-probe excludes 512, finds A's **#501** → adopts #501, abandons its own #512. B's acks point at **#501**.

Result: the tags **split across two open digests**, both issues stay open carrying a ledger marker with this PR's `originPr`, and the two PR ledger stamps are **contradictory** — A's stamp says «duplicate issue #501 was abandoned», B's stamp says «duplicate issue #512 was abandoned». No unambiguous survivor designation exists, and a part-3 sweep honoring both echoes could retire **both** digests. This falsifies the contract stated in this very hunk's doc comment — *"Dedup runs POST-create too — the concurrent digest wins, ours is abandoned (digestDuplicate), and the tags converge on ONE digest"* — and re-opens the record-divergence class the round-1 race finding was closed against (that fix covered the replay pick; the adoption branch was not given the same determinism).

Issue numbers are monotonic in creation order, so one comparison restores the invariant — adopt only the **earlier** filing (the same tie-break the replay pick uses):

```js
if (racedNumber && racedNumber < created) {
    // a concurrent funnel filed EARLIER than ours (between our probe and our
    // create, or before our probe — index lag): adopt theirs, abandon ours
    ...
}
// racedNumber >= created → we filed first: keep ours; the later funnel's own
// post-probe will adopt OUR digest and abandon theirs
```

Both lag orderings then converge on the earliest (lowest) digest with exactly one abandoner — consistent with the replay pick and with no contradictory stamp pair. The existing «post-create probe finding only our own digest keeps ours» test stays green (the own digest is excluded → `racedNumber` is null). Please add a symmetric-race test (both probes see both issues) asserting both funnels converge on the lowest number and only one `digestDuplicate` is ever set.

